BODY = """
<h3>Two decisions that frame frontline care</h3>
<p>For two decades, frontline myeloma therapy has turned on two decisions: whether the patient is a candidate for autologous transplant, and how intensive and how long the treatment around it should be. The answers have shifted as each new drug class moved from relapse into first line.</p>

<h3>Transplant-eligible patients</h3>
<p>High-dose melphalan with autologous transplant became standard when MRC Myeloma VII improved complete responses (44% vs 8%) and median survival (54.1 vs 42.3 months) over conventional chemotherapy [[MRC Myeloma VII (Child)]]; a second (tandem) transplant helped mainly patients without a very good partial response after the first [[IFM 94: single vs tandem transplant (Attal)]]. Induction then improved: bortezomib-dexamethasone replaced VAD [[IFM 2005-01: bortezomib-dex induction (Harousseau)]] and the VTD triplet nearly tripled complete or near-complete responses compared with thalidomide-dexamethasone [[GIMEMA VTD vs TD (Cavo)]].</p>
<p>With better induction, the value of transplant was retested. In IFM 2009, transplant after VRd extended median PFS from 36 to 50 months without changing 4-year survival [[IFM 2009 (Attal)]]; EMN02 found a similar PFS advantage over bortezomib-based intensification [[EMN02/HO95 (Cavo)]]; and the US DETERMINATION trial, with lenalidomide until progression, showed median PFS of 67.5 vs 46.2 months and nearly identical 5-year survival (80.7% vs 79.2%) [[DETERMINATION (Richardson)]]. Transplant therefore remains standard, while deferral is a reasonable choice for some patients if stem cells are collected.</p>
<p>The anti-CD38 quadruplet era began with CASSIOPEIA, where daratumumab added to VTd raised MRD negativity from 44% to 64% [[CASSIOPEIA (Moreau)]]. GRIFFIN brought daratumumab-VRd into US practice [[GRIFFIN (Voorhees)]], and PERSEUS confirmed it: 4-year PFS 84.3% vs 67.7% (HR 0.42) [[PERSEUS (Sonneveld)]]. Isatuximab-VRd raised MRD negativity after induction in GMMG-HD7 [[GMMG-HD7 (Goldschmidt)]]. Carfilzomib-based regimens deepened responses further: KRd induction and carfilzomib-lenalidomide maintenance prolonged PFS [[FORTE (Gay)]], isatuximab-KRd increased MRD negativity at 10⁻⁶ (68% vs 48%) [[EMN24 IsKia (Gay)]], and MASTER tested MRD-guided stopping, which worked in standard-risk but not ultra-high-risk disease [[MASTER (Costa)]].</p>

<h3>Transplant-ineligible patients</h3>
<p>For older patients, adding thalidomide to melphalan-prednisone extended median survival from 33.2 to 51.6 months [[IFM 99-06: MPT (Facon)]], and bortezomib-melphalan-prednisone improved time to progression [[VISTA (San Miguel)]]. Continuous lenalidomide-dexamethasone then provided an alkylator-free backbone [[FIRST (Benboubker)]], and adding bortezomib (VRd) improved both PFS and overall survival (median 75 vs 64 months) [[SWOG S0777 (Durie)]]. Substituting carfilzomib for bortezomib did not help in standard-risk patients [[ECOG E1A11 ENDURANCE (Kumar)]].</p>
<p>Anti-CD38 antibodies changed outcomes again. Daratumumab improved PFS with VMP [[ALCYONE (Mateos)]] and, with lenalidomide-dexamethasone in MAIA, improved overall survival (HR 0.68), a regimen that remains a standard for older or frail patients [[MAIA (Facon)]]. For fit patients not proceeding to transplant, quadruplets followed: isatuximab-VRd in patients up to 80 years achieved 5-year PFS of 63.2% vs 45.2% [[IMROZ (Facon)]], and daratumumab-VRd raised MRD negativity from 39.4% to 60.9% [[CEPHEUS (Usmani)]].</p>

<h3>Maintenance</h3>
<p>Lenalidomide maintenance after transplant nearly doubled time to progression in CALGB 100104 [[CALGB 100104 (McCarthy)]] and PFS in IFM 2005-02, at the cost of more second primary cancers [[IFM 2005-02 (Attal)]]; Myeloma XI showed benefit with and without transplant [[Myeloma XI maintenance (Jackson)]]. A 2026 trial in standard-risk patients without upfront transplant found no survival difference between indefinite and 2-year lenalidomide (7-year overall survival 68.6% vs 69.0%) [[Continuous vs fixed-duration lenalidomide (Kumar)]].</p>

<h3>Risk and response</h3>
<p>Staging moved from the International Staging System [[International Staging System (Greipp)]] to the Revised ISS, which added cytogenetics and LDH [[Revised ISS (Palumbo)]], and then to R2-ISS [[R2-ISS (D'Agostino)]]. In 2025, an IMS/IMWG consensus proposed a shared genomic definition of high risk [[IMS/IMWG high-risk consensus (Avet-Loiseau)]]. Response assessment now centers on MRD: in a meta-analysis, MRD negativity was associated with better PFS (HR 0.41) and survival (HR 0.57), and in April 2024 the FDA's advisory committee voted unanimously to support MRD as an accelerated-approval endpoint [[MRD meta-analysis (Munshi)]].</p>

<h3>Where we are now</h3>
<p>NCCN (version 5.2026) and the 2026 ASCO-Ontario living guideline, summarized here at a high level (regimen-specific NCCN placement not checked), recommend:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Transplant-eligible patients:</b> anti-CD38 quadruplet induction (daratumumab-VRd, or isatuximab-VRd where approved), autologous transplant, and lenalidomide maintenance, with daratumumab continued as in PERSEUS; carfilzomib-containing maintenance may be considered in selected high-risk patients.</li>
<li><b>Fit transplant-ineligible or deferred patients:</b> anti-CD38 quadruplets (isatuximab-VRd or daratumumab-VRd).</li>
<li><b>Older or frail patients:</b> daratumumab-lenalidomide-dexamethasone.</li>
<li><b>Everyone:</b> staging with R-ISS or R2-ISS, high-risk assessment by genomics, and MRD monitoring at defined time points.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Open questions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Transplant in deep responders:</b> can patients who are MRD-negative after a quadruplet safely skip or defer transplant?</li>
<li><b>Duration:</b> can MRD guide when to stop maintenance?</li>
<li><b>High-risk disease:</b> outcomes remain inferior despite quadruplets and intensified maintenance.</li>
<li><b>Choice of antibody and backbone:</b> daratumumab versus isatuximab, and bortezomib versus carfilzomib.</li>
<li><b>Immunotherapy in first line:</b> whether CAR-T or bispecific antibodies should replace transplant or maintenance.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is coming</h3>
<p>Ongoing phase 3 trials (listed under Trials to watch at the end of this page) test CAR-T in first line (CARTITUDE-5 and CARTITUDE-6), bispecific antibodies in induction and maintenance (MajesTEC-7, MagnetisMM-6, MajesTEC-4, MagnetisMM-7), belantamab with lenalidomide (DREAMM-10), iberdomide maintenance, fixed-duration versus continuous anti-CD38 therapy, and response-adapted quadruplets.</p>
"""
